  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-20",
    "title": "V-G-R",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  On November 17, fans of interstellar spaceflight around  planet Earth  were  invited to shine a message  into the night sky. Traced by a flashlight beam switched on and off, the dashes and dots of a Morse code message captured in this time exposure from the Emirates Astronomical Observatory, are read V-G-R. That's a call sign for the  Voyager 1 spacecraft  currently in interstellar space some 26 billion kilometers away. How long did it take the message to reach humanity's most distant  interstellar spacecraft ? Traveling at the speed of light,  the answer is one day . In fact, the Voyager 1 spacecraft was confirmed to reach its  one light-day distance milestone  on November 18, at 10:16:07 UTC, nearly 50 years after launch in 1977. For extra credit, fans of math can calculate that at one light-day per 50 years, Voyager 1 will reach its one light-year (365 light-days) distance milestone in about 18,250 years.   Tomorrow's picture:  pixels in space",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/IMG_4364N_Kyriazis.jpg?w=3898&h=4724&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-20-v-g-r/",
    "credit": " Apostolos Kyriazis ",
    "alt": "Time exposure of a flashlight beam tracing out Morse code for the letters V - G - R.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-20T09:38:24.215695+00:00"
